  "worked_solution": "Since $5$ and $12$ are coprime, then according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $5\\cdot 12=60.$\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\\,⟹\\,x=2+5t,\\,t\\in Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡3 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 2+5t & ≡3 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 5t & ≡1 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $12$ is a relatively small modulus, by trial and error, we have that $5\\cdot 5≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12).$ So, we multiply both sides by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5t & ≡1 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 5\\cdot 5t & ≡5 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ t & ≡5 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $t=5+12s,$ where $s\\in Z.$ Substituting this into the expression for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2+5t \\\\  & =2+5(5+12s) \\\\  & =27+60s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x≡27\\,(\\text{mod}\\,60)$ is the unique solution modulo $60,$ and $x=27$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system.",
